  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale: We use a multi-start, multi-grid optimization approach. Coarser grids (e.g., n=100) are easier to optimize and help us find initial candidates, then we refine them on finer grids (e.g., n=400). For each grid, we generate multiple random initializations and apply SLSQP with the integral constraint. This leverages the lower dimensionality of coarser grids to escape local minima and find globally better solutions.\n\n 2. Mathematical specification:\n    - Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n for grid size n\n    - Minimize: C\u2085 = (2/n) * max_{k} || h * (1 - h_shifted) ||\u2081 (approximated by cross-correlation)\n    - Constraints: \u2211h = n/2; 0 \u2264 h\u1d62 \u2264 1\n\n 3. Implementation procedure:\n    Step 1: Convert initial_h_values if n_matches (else random)\n    Step 2: Run SLSQP with:\n        - Objective: cross-correlation max\n        - Bounds: [0,1]\n        - Equality constraint: sum(h) = n/2\n        - Multi-starts: 4 random points\n    Step 3: Use project_feasible to ensure \u2211h=0.5n\n    Step 4: Score = (2/n) * max(corr)\n\n 4. Search choices:\n    - Grids: [400, 300, 200, 100] (largest first)\n    - Starts per grid: 4\n    - max_iter_base = 2000 (per optimizer run)\n\n 5. Budget and timing:\n    - Total time \u2248 10s/grid (4 grids \u00d7 ~15% each)\n    - Multi-start uses early termination (dt=5% of max_iter)\n\n 6. Return contract:\n    - h_values: clipped to [0,1]\n    - c5_bound: < 0.3808",
